 until the 2040s for that it's the  electrification work that comes sooner  but even on that that part of the  transparent electrification was planned  anyway the middle and mainline  electrification was canned in 2017 um by  chris grayling so i thought it was an  interesting approach given the scale and  palpable anger from across the board in  the north this morning that grant chaps  this morning uh seemingly refusing that  anything will be less good than what was  promised yeah and chris grayling might  well be looking to see if any royalties  are coming his way after the  announcement yesterday for now though  rob we'll speak to you a bit later in  the program for now thanks very much  indeed  but let's return to cricket and  representative of all 18 first-class  counties as well as the england and  wales cricket board will be meeting at  the oval in the wake of the racism  scandal that has engulfed the sport uh  former cricketer azeem rafiq gave a  devastating account to mps this week of  the racism he endured at yorkshire  county cricket club but yesterday he  himself apologized for sending  anti-semitic messages in 2011 in a post  on social media joining us now the  writer and broadcaster meter goes who is  also the author of an upcoming book  entitled the impossible dream can we  ever have a non-racial sports world may  hear good to see you this morning do  hope you're well  very well thank you thank you um can we  start with with the latest development  in a story that frankly by my reckoning  has been going for a number of decades  let alone months and weeks uh azeem  rafiq apologizing for his own use of  anti-semitic language what did you make  of that  well i think  you know  these things have happened in the past  azeem rafiq himself if you like went  into the past brought out a number of  allegations which have proved as you say  devastating and which of course extend  beyond him and he himself is of course  not blameless and and this past hasn't  been looked at and i suspect but more of  this will come out of many other  cricketers and many other  people in the game and and we shouldn't  be surprised that we will get more  revelations in the in the days and weeks  ahead  um here i will freely admit that i am  not a cricket man be on a couple of  boozy days at headingley and watching  scotland beat england in the one day  world cup my experience is pretty  limited you have spent far more time at  these grounds just how pervasive in your  mind has racism been in cricket  well what's interesting about cricket is  not that the spectators generally are a  problem which is not the case in  football which hasn't been historically  the case football where when i was first  reporting football in the 70s national  front used to be outside the ground  saying get your color supplement and  things like that  what has happened in qriket and this is  the big thing about qriket is that it  has gone backwards in the sense when i  first started reporting quicker in the  70s and 80s cricket was a very very  multicultural multi-racial sport england  was the place everybody came to play  cricket and  viv rich's great friend was ian both him  you know one man from antigua another  man couldn't be more english and things  like that cricket at the at the at that  level and the county level has actually  become very white not a deliberate  policy but because this is how it's  happened and that that is the question  and therefore dressing rooms are very  very white the odd non-white person  stands out and of course remarks are  made and things like that and one must  say that over the years i've heard  cricketers tell me privately of the  discrimination and the racism they face  but they were not prepared to come out  because they were not they were worried  this might affect their career how it  would affect their standing and so on  and so forth and this is the change if  you're like this new generation and who  who've grown up in the 