ity to an emergency of such a  scale and says that the government must  learn lessons to strengthen national  resilience we're joined now by gareth  davis from the national audit office  gareth very good morning to you and take  us through your findings the report as i  as i understand it identifies that that  this government like frankly other  governments around the world had a  failure of risk assessment  that's right i mean clearly the the same  findings do apply in many countries  around the world um but it's clear that  uh  in the uk we weren't prepared for a  pandemic of of this kind uh with these  kind of effects  and um i mean one of the reasons that's  clear is that uh this is our 23rd report  on the government's response to code 19  and so we've looked at the furlough  scheme the uh dealing with the impacts  of school closures  and across the whole range of responses  but quite commonly one of our findings  has been there was no plan  to to deal with this kind of situation  so there was no plan for  um how to deal with mass school closures  there was no plan for employment support  schemes so the furlough scheme had to be  developed from scratch in just a very  short number of weeks  so  the question that that raises is what is  it about the way that the risk planning  for these kind of events was carried out  that left us unprepared and as you say  really crucial that the lessons are  learned in case of something of this  kind um or some other kind of  catastrophic a catastrophic event that  needs systematic government-wide  planning  what insight then have you gleaned as to  why we were so singularly unprepared for  this for a coronavirus pandemic there  has been some work of course done in the  past on influenza pandemics but of  course you know transmissibility varies  asymptomatic transmissibility is a  bigger issue when it comes to  coronavirus and that was a bit of an  issue of course when it came to our our  strategizing for this  of course and there's also a good  detailed plan for a mother's outbreak  which would be you know a more a more  infectious a more fatal  outbreak of a disease so leading to the  creation of emergency centers for  concentrating those patients and so on  but but neither that nor the influenza  plan turned out to be exactly what was  required in this case and of course this  is all with the benefit of hindsight but  um  but it's really important that that high  insight is used to understand how the  planning system needs to change give us  a better chance of being prepared uh for  for these kind of  diseases it's also the case you know our  report sets out that the number of  exercises that government carried out  and to test its influenza plan and its  mers plan for example uh not all the  lessons of those exercises were then  implemented in in full some were and  some of the reasons we were able to move  quickly was because of those exercises  but there were gaps and so one of our  recommendations is that government has a  much more systematic method of ensuring  that all of the lessons emerging from uh  big exercises of that kind are  implemented across government  on whose shoulders then does blame if  any is to be apportioned  then fall i mean quite clearly you know  since 2005 we've been conducting these  risk assessments is it the cabinet  office is it other senior figures within  the civil service is it ministers  perhaps  i mean this is a system-wide challenge  so and i report the purpose of our  report is not to apportion blame there's  clearly going to be a public inquiry uh  next year  and it's not our job to anticipate what  a public inquiry might find our job is  to accumulate the evidence from our  audit work and to present that in a  factual and balanced way so this report  is part of that effort  um but i think the  in response to your question about where  does responsibility lie it's it's across  the whole system so cabinet office has  lead responsibility for risk 